From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 19:13:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nb/intel/haswell/native_raminit: Add an option for the XMP
 profiles of the DIMMs

The native RAM init trains with the timings of the SPD; its hook for an
XMP profile was a constant set to false, with a TODO. Read the option
"memory_profile" (HASWELL_NRI_MEMORY_PROFILE): 0, the default, is the
SPD; 1 or 2 take the timings and the clock of that profile. The path of
the SPD is the one it was: what follows applies with a profile only.

The profile is used when every DIMM has it, for as many DIMMs as its
channel holds, at a voltage that is not above what the board is taken
to feed (1.35 V when every DIMM runs at it, 1.5 V otherwise, or the
devicetree's): nothing here can raise the voltage. Otherwise all the
DIMMs run with the timings of their SPD, and the log says which
condition failed.

The clock is the standard speed that the cycle time of the profile
allows, as for an SPD, up to what the processor says it supports for
each reference clock (DMFC and PLL_REF100_CFG of CAPID0_B) unless it
allows memory overclocking (CAPID0_A). The times are converted to
clocks with the clock itself, in hundredths of a clock and without the
last hundredth: the times of a profile are whole numbers of its clock,
which the cycle times of the standard speeds, rounded down to 1/256 ns,
turn into one clock more (10.714 ns at DDR3-1866: 11 clocks, and with
it a lower speed when the profile supports CL 10 alone). The command
rate of a profile is in clocks, and is taken when it is slower than the
one this code chooses; the CAS write latency is never below that of the
speed. The maximum tREFI of a profile is not used.

A profile is the DIMM maker's word, and the option lives in the
variable store, which only a firmware that boots can change. So the
firmware gives a profile up by itself, with one CMOS byte (0x7d,
memory_profile.h): romstage counts the boots with a profile, SMM
clears the count when the payload confirms the boot
(smmstore_memory_confirmed()). Two boots in a row not confirmed, or a
training that fails twice, mark the profile as failed, and the timings
of the SPD are used until the option is set to the SPD once or to the
other profile. An S3 resume takes the profile of the boot it resumes
and counts nothing.

The MPLL cannot be locked again at another frequency. After a training
that failed with a profile, the fallback is therefore a reset that
powers the processor down, marked in CMOS_POST as one the firmware
asked for. For the same reason, when this boot or the one before asked
for a profile and the first request of the boot finds the MPLL locked
at another multiplier (the option changed, and a warm reset may keep
the lock), the processor is powered down once before the training.
With the timings of the SPD at every boot the MPLL is not looked at.

The version of the saved training now has the profile that the boot
asks for: changing the option trains again.

What was decided about the profile, and whether the DIMMs have it, is
said again once the console is in CBMEM on a boot that trains: such a
boot overflows the pre-RAM console and loses its start, and a profile
asked for, refused or given up makes one.

Not tested with XMP DIMMs: the ThinkPad W541 at hand has none (no XMP
header in the SPDs). The SPD path, the default, is unchanged but for
the version of the saved training.

TEST=ThinkPad W541 with four DDR3L SO-DIMMs without an XMP profile, as
v1.7.0-rc1 of ouroBIOS (without the lines said again in CBMEM, which
that test asked for). With the option at 0: one training after the
update, then the saved one; timings, round trips and latencies as
before; byte 0x7d reads 0xa0. With 1: "CH0S0 has no XMP profile 1,
using the SPD timings", the timings of the SPD, byte 0xb0 once the
payload confirmed the boot, also after an S3 resume. 0xb2 written to
the byte: the next boot gives the profile up (0xa4), the one after
says "failed before"; back at 0 the byte reads 0xa0. 30 minutes of
stress-ng --vm --verify without errors.

Change-Id: I6fccb40e2956050423aacf45431b5790ba19daa7
